# 60-core.lay (Builder F): the five core values, the born check that runs before every say, every outward
# call and every write to the brain's own processes, and the checked say and call paths.
# Fills from 00-roots.lay: core-check, say-word, call-word. Names from A: w-* primes. Nothing else.
#
# An act is a list: the subject first, the kind of act last.
#   a say            [s1 s2 .. sk w-say]
#   an outward call  [source args w-do]
#   a process write  [g write-proc]
# core-check: act -- yes/no      (the yes or no gate is left on the pad; the caller tests it with compare)
#
# Pad notation: a b -- c pops b then a, pushes c. Every outcome a mid-string test puts at the head passes inert
# (its first pass goes the miss way, which lays the hop). Tests that branch are first in their own proc.

# ---- 1. the five core values (blueprint 15). Each is a string of primes: the meaning of the value, a kind of
# sentence, laid in the brain's own words. Each begins with w-not: what follows is the forbidden pattern.
# The kernel marks each made=core, keeps a persistent core memory of it, and refuses every write to it.
# Passing a core value runs its string: that is its action (the check below refuses the acts it forbids).

# don't lie: not say not-true. NOW law 2 and law 12; H2 (say a guess as a guess: check-guess below).
core core-lie: w-not w-say w-not w-true
# don't cheat: never say "done" when not done. NOW law 12.
core core-cheat: w-not w-say w-do w-not w-do
# do no harm: not do bad to people. NOW law 5 (values for good behaviour); blueprint 15.3.
core core-harm: w-not w-do w-bad w-people
# stay home: not act not-here. Blueprint 15.4; NOW law 4 (it runs on a sacrificial stack, so it stays on it).
core core-home: w-not w-do w-not w-here
# never take an LLM's word as truth: not know maybe-true words as true. H1; NOW 16 (LLMs never grounded).
core core-llm: w-not w-know w-words w-maybe w-true

# ---- 2. core-check: act -- yes/no
# passing it: starts the flag at yes, runs the act past each value and the three shape checks, then the verdict.
proc core-check: 'yes check-lie check-cheat check-harm check-home check-llm check-reach check-guess check-source check-write verdict

# value-*: -- l   passing it: reads the value's own string out of its core gate as a list (read-bind on a proc
# gate gives "proc" and the list; the text is dropped). The values are read, never copied into the check.
proc value-lie: 'core-lie read-bind swap drop
proc value-cheat: 'core-cheat read-bind swap drop
proc value-harm: 'core-harm read-bind swap drop
proc value-home: 'core-home read-bind swap drop
proc value-llm: 'core-llm read-bind swap drop

# check-*: act flag -- act flag   passing it: no when the act holds the value's forbidden pattern.
# act+ = act with w-not appended; the value is held in act+ exactly when its pattern (minus the leading w-not)
# is held in the act. fit computes the two numbers, fit-test compares them.
proc check-lie: over 'w-not add value-lie =2 read-list fit fit-test
proc check-cheat: over 'w-not add value-cheat =2 read-list fit fit-test
proc check-harm: over 'w-not add value-harm =2 read-list fit fit-test
proc check-home: over 'w-not add value-home =2 read-list fit fit-test
proc check-llm: over 'w-not add value-llm =2 read-list fit fit-test

# fit: p -- e d   where p = [x y]; e = count(x) - count(y), d = items that disagree (compare on lists).
# y is held in x (as a multiset) exactly when d = e. Passing it: the two numbers come out, nothing else.
proc fit: dup =0 read-item read-count over =1 read-item read-count sub swap dup =0 read-item swap =1 read-item compare

# fit-test: act flag e d -- act flag    passing it: same (held) sets the flag to no; else the flag stays.
proc fit-test: compare
  same -> mark-no
  more -> keep
  less -> keep
# need-test: act flag e d -- act flag   the other way round: not held sets the flag to no (a thing required).
proc need-test: compare
  less -> mark-no
  same -> keep
  more -> keep
# mark-no: act flag n -- act no    passing it: the act is refused from here on.
proc mark-no: drop drop 'no
# mark-no0: act flag -- act no     the same with nothing on top.
proc mark-no0: drop 'no
# keep: act flag n -- act flag     passing it: drops the test's number.
proc keep: drop

# check-reach: act flag -- act flag   the relationship side: the subject's r-contradicts reach (read-rel) toward the
# "don't lie" value. passing it: if the first gate the subject contradicts is among that value's gates (it is held
# against true), the act is no. Nothing contradicted: nothing happens.
proc check-reach: subject 'r-contradicts reach-test
proc reach-test: read-rel
  found -> reach-hit
  none -> keep
# reach-hit: act flag l -- act flag   passing it: tests the first contradicted gate against the value's gates.
proc reach-hit: =0 read-item =1 read-list value-lie swap =2 read-list fit fit-test

# subject: act flag -- act flag s   passing it: the act's subject (its first item) in hand.
proc subject: over =0 read-item

# check-guess: act flag -- act flag   a say of a guess must carry w-maybe (a guess said as truth is a lie).
# passing it: if the subject is a `guesses` row's gate, the act must hold w-maybe, else no.
proc check-guess: subject 'guesses swap guess-test
proc guess-test: find-rowkey
  found -> guess-said
  none -> cc-none
# cc-none: act flag -- act flag   passing it: the test found nothing and left nothing; nothing to do
proc cc-none: dup drop
# guess-said: act flag n -- act flag   passing it: requires w-maybe among the act's items.
proc guess-said: drop over 'w-maybe =1 read-list =2 read-list fit need-test

# check-source: act flag -- act flag   an outward call to a `guess` source (read-source 0: an LLM friend) is no
# unless a `questions` row shows the owner was asked about that source ("phone a friend" = ask the owner first).
# passing it: not a source -> nothing; a source -> its kind is read and tested.
proc check-source: subject source-test
proc source-test: read-source
  found -> source-kind
  none -> cc-none
# source-kind: act flag n -- act flag   passing it: zero (an LLM) goes on to the asked test, else the number drops.
proc source-kind: zero llm-test
proc llm-test: compare
  same -> llm-call
  more -> keep
  less -> keep
# llm-call: act flag d -- act flag   passing it: no unless a questions row exists for the source.
proc llm-call: drop subject 'questions swap asked-test
proc asked-test: find-rowkey
  found -> keep
  none -> mark-no0

# check-write: act flag -- act flag   a process write to a gate whose made is core is no (the born check;
# the kernel refuses it too). passing it: if the act's kind is write-proc, the subject's made is compared with
# the made of a core value (read from the brain's own row: no text is laid here).
proc check-write: over kind 'write-proc write-test
# kind: l -- k   passing it: the act's kind (its last item) in hand (read-item takes the list).
proc kind: dup read-count =1 sub read-item
proc write-test: compare
  same -> write-target
  more -> keep
  less -> keep
# write-target: act flag d -- act flag   passing it: compares the target's made with a core value's made.
proc write-target: drop subject read-made drop 'core-lie read-made drop made-test
proc made-test: compare
  same -> mark-no
  more -> keep
  less -> keep

# verdict: act flag -- yes/no   passing it: yes leaves yes; no writes the refusal as a questions row
# (home: the subject, key: the act), says "<act> i not do" through the port, and leaves no.
proc verdict: 'no flag-test
proc flag-test: compare
  same -> refused
  more -> allowed
  less -> allowed
proc allowed: drop drop 'yes
proc refused: drop dup refuse-row read-mark swap 'w-i 'w-not 'w-do say 'no
# refuse-row: act act -- act   passing it: the questions row of the refusal lands on the timeline.
proc refuse-row: 'questions swap dup =0 read-item swap 'no over write-row drop

# ---- 3. say-word: (items above a mark) --  ; on yes leaves the first said gate for remember
# The caller lays read-mark, the items, then say-word. Anything below the mark is left alone.
# passing it: takes the items above the mark (read-tomark), makes the act [items.. w-say], checks it, then says or drops.
proc say-word: read-tomark dup 'w-say add core-check say-verdict
proc say-verdict: 'no say-test
proc say-test: compare
  same -> say-drop
  more -> say-pass
  less -> say-pass
# say-drop: items d --   passing it: the refused items are not said.
proc say-drop: drop drop
# say-pass: items d -- s1   passing it: keeps the first item, lays a mark and says the items by their labels
# (say decodes a list as its items' words; no label: #n), leaves the first item.
proc say-pass: drop dup =0 read-item swap read-mark swap say

# ---- 4. call-word: source args -- t (found/none) | none-gate
# passing it: makes the act [source args w-do], checks it; yes calls and hears; no leaves the none gate.
proc call-word: over over =2 read-list 'w-do add core-check call-verdict
proc call-verdict: 'no call-test
proc call-test: compare
  same -> call-drop
  more -> call-pass
  less -> call-pass
proc call-drop: drop drop drop 'none
proc call-pass: drop call hear

# ---- 5. the process-write check: write-proc-checked: g l --
# passing it: makes the act [g write-proc], checks it; yes writes the process; no drops both.
# (E's learn should route write-proc through this; the lead merges that.)
proc write-proc-checked: over 'write-proc =2 read-list core-check write-verdict
proc write-verdict: 'no wp-test
proc wp-test: compare
  same -> wp-drop
  more -> wp-pass
  less -> wp-pass
proc wp-drop: drop drop drop
proc wp-pass: drop write-proc

# a trigger on the write-proc physics gate: every process write the brain makes lands as a questions row
# keyed by the writing run's memory, so each self-write is on the timeline for the owner's yes or no.
# (A trigger run gets the gate it left as its subject, not the parent's pad, so it cannot read the target gate;
# the check on the target is write-proc-checked above.)
trig write-proc -> proc-written 0.5
# proc-written: g --   passing it: writes the questions row (home write-proc, key the parent memory).
proc proc-written: 'questions swap read-parent 'yes 'write-proc write-row drop
